window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Physical Requirements", "chapter": "Physical Quantum Computation", "url": "01-physical-quantum-computation.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Physical Requirements A circuit says to rotate a qubit, couple it to another, and measure. A laboratory must supply a physical state, a timed interaction, and a detector. This course follows that chain and asks what must be true for the measured bit to mean what the algorithm predicts. 1.1.1 Encoding and initialization Choose two physical states to represent zero and one, and a procedure that prepares a known starting state. The carrier usually has additional states, motion, and environmental couplings. A usable encoding must remain identifiable throughout the operation. Merely observing two distinguishable detector signals is not yet evidence of coherent control between the corresponding states. 1.1.2 Control and measurement A gate is implemented by controlled evolution; readout converts a state-dependent response into a classical record. DiVincenzo’s framework organizes the requirements around identifiable scalable qubits, initialization, adequate coherence, universal control, and measurement, with additional requirements for communication. These are linked engineering tasks. Improving isolation may make control or measurement more difficult, so a device must be evaluated as a whole. Problem 1.1. Does a long coherence time alone make a good processor? Worked solution No. Initialization, control, coupling, readout, and scalable operation must also work. A perfectly isolated but inaccessible state cannot execute a useful program."}, {"title": "Computational Resources", "chapter": "Physical Quantum Computation", "url": "01-physical-quantum-computation.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Computational Resources 1.2.1 Physical and logical qubits A physical qubit is a degree of freedom used to store a two-state system. A logical qubit is encoded across physical resources with a specified protection and control scheme. Counting physical devices does not determine how many reliable logical operations they support. Some encodings use several two-level systems; others use many states of an oscillator. Both require an explicit error model and recovery procedure. 1.2.2 Time, connectivity, and error Consider an illustrative gate duration of 100 ns and an exponential coherence time of 100 μs. Their ratio is 0.001, but this is not a measured gate error. The relevant error also depends on which state is stored, the noise process, pulse imperfections, and leakage. For excited-state decay alone, the probability of a decay during that interval is \\(1-e^{-t/T_1}\\approx t/T_1=0.001\\). A phase-sensitive superposition asks a different question. Problem 1.2. What assumption permits the estimate t/T1? Worked solution An exponential relaxation model and a duration much shorter than T1. It estimates decay of excited-state population, not every contribution to gate infidelity."}, {"title": "Implementation Models", "chapter": "Physical Quantum Computation", "url": "01-physical-quantum-computation.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Implementation Models 1.3.1 Gate and analog evolution Gate computation uses a sequence of calibrated transformations. Analog simulation directly engineers an interacting generator and observes its evolution; annealing changes a generator along a path. These models share control and readout problems but require different correctness tests. A controllable analog device is not automatically a universal gate processor, and a circuit description alone does not specify its physical implementation. 1.3.2 A device-level specification For each device, write an implementation contract: the encoded states, preparation error, control Hamiltonian and approximation, accessible interactions, readout rule, timing, and dominant failure modes. Compare the measured behavior with the ideal contract before discussing scaling. Numerical examples in this course are deliberately illustrative rather than current vendor specifications. Problem 1.3. Why distinguish physical and logical qubit counts? Worked solution Encoding, syndrome measurement, routing, and auxiliary-state preparation consume physical resources. The mapping depends on the code and target logical error. We begin by asking when a physical system with many states can be approximated by a qubit. Sources and further study: DiVincenzo, The Physical Implementation of Quantum Computation ."}, {"title": "Energy Levels", "chapter": "Physical Qubits", "url": "02-physical-qubits.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Energy Levels A device has energy levels E0, E1, and E2. We want to rotate only the lowest two. The third level matters even when the circuit drawing has only one wire: a drive can accidentally put amplitude there. 2.1.1 Stationary states and frequencies An energy eigenstate evolves by a phase \\(e^{-iEt/\\hbar}\\), where ℏ is Planck’s constant divided by 2π. For a superposition of two levels, the relative phase advances at angular frequency \\(\\omega_{01}=(E_1-E_0)/\\hbar\\). Ordinary frequency is \\(f_{01}=\\omega_{01}/2\\pi\\). A common energy offset changes only a global phase. We will state whether frequencies are quoted in cycles per second or radians per second. 2.1.2 The two-level approximation The qubit approximation keeps two chosen levels and neglects the rest. This requires both a preparation confined to that subspace and operations that approximately preserve it. A harmonic oscillator has equally spaced transitions; a drive resonant with its first transition can also excite the next. Anharmonicity, the difference between neighboring transition frequencies, can permit selective addressing. Unequal transition frequencies allow spectral selection of a two-level subspace. Lines are schematic. Problem 2.1. If E1−E0=hf, what relative phase accumulates in time 1/(2f)? Worked solution The excited component gains exp(−iπ)=−1 relative to the ground component. This is half a cycle, not half a radian."}, {"title": "Qubit Encodings", "chapter": "Physical Qubits", "url": "02-physical-qubits.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 Qubit Encodings 2.2.1 Computational subspaces An encoding need not use the two lowest energy states. Hyperfine spin states, two optical paths, or a protected multi-system subspace can serve as zero and one. The choice affects susceptibility to noise and which controls connect the states. A well-defined encoding specifies a basis, a phase reference, and the larger state space in which errors may occur. 2.2.2 Leakage and selection rules Leakage is amplitude outside the computational subspace, not simply a wrong zero or one. A selection rule can suppress a transition because the control operator has zero matrix element between the relevant levels. Such protection is conditional on the symmetry and control fields. If an unwanted transition is detuned by Δ and coupled with strength Ω, a simple two-level model bounds its population by \\(\\Omega^2/(\\Omega^2+\\Delta^2)\\); this is a useful warning, not a universal multilevel leakage formula. Problem 2.2. Does a zero computational-basis error guarantee no leakage? Worked solution No. If leaked outcomes are ignored or misclassified, the reported bit statistics can conceal population outside the chosen subspace."}, {"title": "Effective Dynamics", "chapter": "Physical Qubits", "url": "02-physical-qubits.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 Effective Dynamics 2.3.1 Hamiltonians and rotating frames In our convention \\(Z=\\ket0\\bra0-\\ket1\\bra1\\), so a system with level one higher in energy has \\(H_0=-\\hbar\\omega_{01}Z/2\\) after removing a common offset. A Hamiltonian is the matrix generating evolution. Moving to a rotating coordinate frame removes the rapid reference phase and exposes the drive’s slow action. Frame changes do not physically rotate the state by themselves; they change its mathematical description. 2.3.2 Testing the approximation Test the approximation by varying drive strength and detuning, checking population in extra levels, and comparing different pulse lengths. A good fit to one oscillation does not certify a closed two-level system over an entire computation. Keep the neglected coupling scales visible when moving from a physical model to a gate specification. Problem 2.3. Why is an equally spaced oscillator not automatically a selective two-level qubit? Worked solution A resonant field can couple several neighboring transitions at the same frequency. A usable encoding needs additional nonlinearity, selection, or control structure. With the subspace specified, we can calculate how a resonant drive rotates its amplitudes."}, {"title": "Driven Transitions", "chapter": "Coherent Control", "url": "03-coherent-control.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Driven Transitions Prepare zero and apply a resonant field for a variable time. The measured excited population oscillates. This experiment turns an abstract X rotation into a calibrated duration and field amplitude. 3.1.1 Resonance and Rabi oscillations In a rotating-frame model, let \\(H_{\\rm rot}=\\hbar(\\Delta Z+\\Omega X)/2\\), with Δ defined as the coefficient of Z in this frame and Ω the on-resonance angular Rabi rate. At Δ=0, evolution is \\(R_x(\\Omega t)=\\cos(\\Omega t/2)I-i\\sin(\\Omega t/2)X\\). Starting from zero gives \\(P_1=\\sin^2(\\Omega t/2)\\). A π pulse has Ωt=π, swapping the populations. 3.1.2 Pulse area and phase If Ω/2π=5 MHz, a square π pulse lasts 100 ns. Changing the drive phase selects a rotation axis in the XY plane: \\(H=\\hbar\\Omega(\\cos\\phi X+\\sin\\phi Y)/2\\) under the chosen phase convention. For a resonant pulse with a fixed axis, the rotation angle is the area \\(\\int\\Omega(t)dt\\). Varying axes in time generally requires ordered evolution rather than a single scalar area. Detuning changes both the oscillation frequency and the maximum excitation probability; the pulse duration alone no longer determines the rotation. Problem 3.1. What is the π/2-pulse duration at a 5 MHz Rabi rate? Worked solution 50 ns. The angular rate is 2π·5 MHz, so Ωt=π/2."}, {"title": "Control Errors", "chapter": "Coherent Control", "url": "03-coherent-control.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Control Errors 3.2.1 Detuning and amplitude error For constant detuning, define \\(\\Omega_R=\\sqrt{\\Omega^2+\\Delta^2}\\). Directly exponentiating the Pauli combination gives \\(P_1(t)=\\Omega^2\\sin^2(\\Omega_Rt/2)/\\Omega_R^2\\). Detuning reduces the maximum population transfer and tilts the rotation axis. A fractional amplitude error ε changes a nominal π angle to π(1+ε), producing population error approximately π²ε²/4 near the intended inversion. That one-input error does not fully characterize the coherent gate error. 3.2.2 Pulse shaping and leakage A shorter pulse needs more bandwidth and stronger drive. That can excite nearby transitions or distort the rotating-wave approximation, which neglects rapidly oscillating terms when the control scales are small relative to the carrier frequency. Smooth envelopes reduce some spectral leakage, but a particular pulse shape must be checked against the actual multilevel system and transfer function. Problem 3.2. If |Δ|=Ω, can a square pulse invert perfectly? Worked solution No. The maximum excited probability in this model is Ω²/(Ω²+Δ²)=1/2."}, {"title": "Gate Calibration", "chapter": "Coherent Control", "url": "03-coherent-control.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Gate Calibration 3.3.1 Rotation experiments A Rabi scan estimates pulse area; repeated small rotations can magnify a calibration error; Ramsey experiments expose detuning. Use independent validation sequences after fitting. A maximum in excited-state probability may be shifted by readout error or drift, and a successful inversion alone does not verify the intended phase on an arbitrary superposition. 3.3.2 Frames and virtual phases A virtual Z updates the phase reference for later controls rather than requiring a separate resonant pulse. It represents a logical Z rotation only if subsequent drive phases and measurement bases consistently use that updated frame. Frame tracking is powerful because it avoids some physical operations, but losing track of a frame produces real relative-phase errors. Problem 3.3. Why is a successful zero-to-one pulse insufficient as a gate test? Worked solution Its phase action on superpositions may be wrong even when the output population is correct. An interference experiment is needed. A single drive supplies rotations. Two-qubit gates require an interaction that cannot be written as separate local evolutions."}, {"title": "Interaction Models", "chapter": "Qubit Interactions", "url": "04-qubit-interactions.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 Interaction Models Two qubits can each be well controlled yet remain computationally separate. To entangle them, the energy or transition of one must depend on the state of the other, or both must interact through a shared mediator. 4.1.1 Conditional energies Consider \\(H=\\hbar J Z\\otimes Z/2\\). Equal-bit states have one energy sign and unequal-bit states the opposite. Evolution for time t gives \\(U=\\mathrm{diag}(e^{-iJt/2},e^{iJt/2},e^{iJt/2},e^{-iJt/2})\\). The combination of phases \\(\\phi_{00}-\\phi_{01}-\\phi_{10}+\\phi_{11}=-2Jt\\) cannot in general be removed by separate single-qubit phases. It measures the nonlocal conditional phase. 4.1.2 Exchange interactions An exchange-type model \\(H=\\hbar g(XX+YY)/2\\) leaves 00 and 11 unchanged and couples 01 to 10. In that two-state subspace it is ℏgX, so \\(\\ket{01}\\) evolves to \\(\\cos(gt)\\ket{01}-i\\sin(gt)\\ket{10}\\). At gt=π/4 it is an entangled state. The coupling’s units and factor-of-two convention must be fixed before translating its strength into a gate time. Problem 4.1. For the exchange model, when is population fully transferred from 01 to 10? Worked solution At gt=π/2, the state is −i|10〉. Half that duration produces equal populations with a relative phase."}, {"title": "Entangling Gates", "chapter": "Qubit Interactions", "url": "04-qubit-interactions.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Entangling Gates 4.2.1 Controlled phases For the ZZ model, Jt=π/2 gives a nonlocal phase of −π. Applying \\(R_z(-\\pi/2)\\) on both qubits afterward makes the operation equal to controlled-Z up to a global phase. This follows by multiplying the four diagonal entries. Local phase corrections are part of the gate specification; saying “wait under ZZ” without them can implement a different circuit. 4.2.2 Mediated interactions A resonator, a shared vibration, or an optical link can mediate coupling. A successful gate must usually return the mediator to a state independent of the logical input. Otherwise the mediator stores which-branch information and the data qubits alone undergo a noisy operation. A closed trajectory of a mediator can leave a useful conditional phase while removing that record. Problem 4.2. Can separate Z rotations erase every ZZ conditional phase? Worked solution No. They change individual phases but leave the nonlocal combination unchanged modulo 2π."}, {"title": "Gate Performance", "chapter": "Qubit Interactions", "url": "04-qubit-interactions.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Gate Performance 4.3.1 Residual entanglement Check both populations and coherence after the interaction. If one branch leaves a mediator in |e0〉 and another in |e1〉, the data coherence is multiplied by their overlap 〈e1|e0〉 when the mediator is ignored. An overlap of zero completely distinguishes those branches even if the measured data populations look correct. 4.3.2 Speed and unwanted coupling Stronger coupling can shorten a gate but increase leakage, sensitivity to control errors, or interactions with spectators. Always-on coupling requires refocusing or frame-aware compensation during nominally independent gates. The useful question is not the largest possible coupling but the fastest operation satisfying the required error and parallelism constraints. Problem 4.3. Why must an auxiliary oscillator close its trajectory? Worked solution If its final state depends on the data, tracing it out reduces coherence. Closing removes the distinguishing record while allowing a geometric phase to remain. We now have candidate gates. Initialization and measurement determine whether their inputs and outputs can be used reliably."}, {"title": "Initialization", "chapter": "Initialization and Readout", "url": "05-initialization-and-readout.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Initialization Two preparations produce overlapping detector signals. Choosing a threshold gives a bit, but it also gives mistakes. Readout is therefore a calibrated statistical decision linked to a physical disturbance. 5.1.1 Thermal preparation For an ideal two-level system in thermal equilibrium with energy gap ΔE, the excited probability is \\(p_1=1/(1+e^{\\Delta E/k_BT})\\). This follows from the ratio p1/p0=exp(−ΔE/kBT) and normalization. Low temperature helps when the gap is large compared with thermal energy, but unwanted excitations and incomplete equilibration can invalidate the equilibrium prediction. 5.1.2 Active reset Active reset measures the state and conditionally applies a correction, or uses a dissipative process designed to remove excitation. Its error includes measurement mistakes, latency, residual population, and leakage. Waiting a fixed multiple of a nominal T1 is another preparation strategy, but it has a time cost and may approach a nonzero thermal excited population rather than perfect zero. Problem 5.1. At ΔE/kBT=5, estimate thermal excited population. Worked solution It is 1/(1+e⁵)≈0.00669. This is an equilibrium two-level prediction, not a universal device initialization error."}, {"title": "Measurement Signals", "chapter": "Initialization and Readout", "url": "05-initialization-and-readout.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Measurement Signals 5.2.1 State-dependent response Readout may detect fluorescence, a resonator phase shift, a charge change, or a photon. In each case state information is converted into distinguishable classical signals. Amplification and averaging can improve separation, while the measurement interaction itself can disturb the state. The probability of assigning a correct bit and the probability of preserving an eigenstate are different properties. 5.2.2 Assignment and backaction Let errors be e0=P(report 1|prepared 0) and e1=P(report 0|prepared 1). The observed probability of one is \\(q=e_0+(1-e_0-e_1)p\\), where p is the actual excited population before readout. If e0=e1=0.02 and p=0.8, q=0.788. Inverting this relation requires reliable calibration and increases uncertainty; it does not turn noisy single-shot labels into known correct outcomes. Problem 5.2. If e0=e1=1/2, can the assignment relation be inverted? Worked solution No. The output is always a fair bit independent of p, so the detector contains no information about the population."}, {"title": "Readout Design", "chapter": "Initialization and Readout", "url": "05-initialization-and-readout.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Readout Design 5.3.1 Integration and discrimination Longer integration collects more signal but also permits decay during measurement, drift, or state changes induced by the readout. Optimize the total assignment error for the intended input distribution and operation. Equal-prior accuracy can be a poor measure for rare-event experiments. A threshold chosen on calibration data should be tested on separate data. 5.3.2 Repeated measurement and reuse A quantum nondemolition readout approximately preserves the measured observable so a repeated measurement gives the same eigenvalue, absent intervening errors. High assignment fidelity alone does not establish this property. Repeated readout and reset experiments are important when a qubit must be measured many times during error correction rather than only once at the end. Problem 5.3. Does a destructive detector with perfect labels qualify as reusable nondemolition readout? Worked solution No. It may identify the original state exactly while consuming the carrier. Reuse and preservation are separate requirements. Noise changes the state even when we intend to do nothing. The next chapter separates energy loss from phase uncertainty."}, {"title": "Relaxation", "chapter": "Noise and Decoherence", "url": "06-noise-and-decoherence.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 Relaxation An excited-state population decays, while a superposition can lose its interference even without losing energy. These two experiments motivate distinct timescales rather than one universal “qubit lifetime.” 6.1.1 Energy loss and T1 In a simple zero-temperature relaxation model, excited population obeys \\(dp_1/dt=-p_1/T_1\\), whose solution is \\(p_1(t)=p_1(0)e^{-t/T_1}\\). The same amplitude-damping process reduces off-diagonal density-matrix elements by \\(e^{-t/(2T_1)}\\). Population and coherence therefore decay at different rates even before additional phase noise is included. 6.1.2 Thermal excitation With upward and downward transition rates Γup and Γdown, the population approaches \\(p_{\\rm eq}=\\Gamma_{\\rm up}/(\\Gamma_{\\rm up}+\\Gamma_{\\rm down})\\). Its return rate is their sum. An observed nonzero late-time population can indicate temperature or other excitation processes; assuming it is zero can bias a fitted relaxation time. Leakage into an extra level needs a larger model. Problem 6.1. Can T2 exceed 2T1 in the stated exponential model? Worked solution No. The pure-dephasing rate is nonnegative. An apparent violation suggests inconsistent fits, definitions, or a model outside these assumptions."}, {"title": "Dephasing", "chapter": "Noise and Decoherence", "url": "06-noise-and-decoherence.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Dephasing 6.2.1 Frequency noise and T2 A fluctuating transition frequency accumulates an uncertain phase. Averaging \\(e^{i\\phi}\\) over repetitions reduces the observed coherence even if every realization is pure. For independent exponential relaxation and Markovian pure dephasing, \\(1/T_2=1/(2T_1)+1/T_\\phi\\). For T1=100 μs and Tφ=100 μs this gives T2≈66.7 μs. A single exponential need not fit slowly drifting or non-Markovian noise. 6.2.2 Echo and noise timescales A π pulse midway through free evolution reverses the effect of a static frequency offset: the phase accumulated before the pulse is canceled by that after it. This spin-echo idea suppresses slowly varying dephasing, not energy decay or arbitrary rapidly changing noise. Ramsey T2* often includes shot-to-shot frequency variation; echo-based times probe a different filter of the noise spectrum. Problem 6.2. Does echo reverse a spontaneous emission event? Worked solution No. Emission transfers energy and information to the environment. A mid-sequence π pulse does not recover the emitted quantum state."}, {"title": "Error Models", "chapter": "Noise and Decoherence", "url": "06-noise-and-decoherence.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Error Models 6.3.1 Leakage and correlated errors A Pauli-error description can be convenient for circuit analysis, but coherent overrotation, leakage, loss, and correlated faults can behave differently. For a repeated overrotation by ε about the same axis, m repetitions accumulate an angle mε before randomization or other dynamics. Treating these as independent stochastic errors can give the wrong scaling. 6.3.2 From a model to a prediction State the noise model, the sequence, and the observable before predicting an error probability. A relaxation measurement during idle does not directly determine error during a strong driven gate. Spectator operations can change noise and crosstalk. Useful models should explain more than the calibration experiment that supplied their parameters. Problem 6.3. After ten identical overrotations by ε, what coherent angle error remains? Worked solution 10ε if the rotations share the same axis and no other action changes the accumulation. A probability estimate requires applying that rotation to the specified input. The next chapter constructs the experiments that infer these parameters and separates inference from proof of gate quality."}, {"title": "Calibration Experiments", "chapter": "Calibration and Characterization", "url": "07-calibration-and-characterization.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Calibration Experiments A fringe oscillates while its envelope decays. Its period estimates a detuning; its envelope estimates a sequence-dependent coherence time. Extracting those quantities requires a model and a calibration strategy. 7.1.1 Spectroscopy and Rabi scans Spectroscopy scans drive frequency and measures the response to locate a transition. A Rabi experiment fixes the drive frequency and scans pulse duration or strength. The two scans answer different questions: where a transition lies and how strongly it is driven. Power broadening, extra levels, and state-preparation error can alter the apparent resonance. 7.1.2 Ramsey and echo In a Ramsey sequence, two π/2 rotations surround a wait. With a suitable readout phase, \\(P_0(t)=[1+V e^{-t/T_2^*}\\cos(\\Delta t+\\phi_0)]/2\\). Visibility V, offset phase φ0, decay, and detuning must not be conflated. A 2 μs fringe period corresponds to |Δ|/2π=0.5 MHz. The sign of detuning requires a phase-sensitive convention, not just the period. Problem 7.1. What does a Ramsey period fail to reveal by itself? Worked solution It gives the magnitude of a detuning in the chosen model, but not its sign, the preparation error, or a full gate fidelity."}, {"title": "Process Characterization", "chapter": "Calibration and Characterization", "url": "07-calibration-and-characterization.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Process Characterization 7.2.1 State and process tomography For a qubit state, measurements along X,Y,Z determine its Bloch components through their ±1 means, yielding ρ=(I+xX+yY+zZ)/2. Process tomography uses several known input states and output measurements to infer a channel. It requires assumptions about preparation and measurement, so an inaccurate reference can be mistaken for a bad gate. 7.2.2 Randomized benchmarking Randomized benchmarking applies random gate sequences and an ideal inverse, then fits survival versus sequence length. Under the standard single-qubit depolarizing model the decay has form Aα^m+B, and average error per Clifford is (1−α)/2. This is a model-dependent interpretation, not a universal identity for arbitrary drift, leakage, or gate-dependent coherent noise. Error per Clifford also differs from error per native gate. Problem 7.2. For α=0.998, what is the single-qubit depolarizing-model error per Clifford? Worked solution It is (1−0.998)/2=0.001. The qualification about the model and the counted gate type is essential."}, {"title": "Measurement Limits", "chapter": "Calibration and Characterization", "url": "07-calibration-and-characterization.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Measurement Limits 7.3.1 Preparation and measurement errors Preparation and measurement errors can appear in fitted offsets, but separating them from gate errors requires appropriate assumptions or additional experiments. A high survival probability after one favorable sequence proves little about all inputs. Test phase-sensitive states, leakage, simultaneous gates, and repeated readout when those behaviors matter to the workload. 7.3.2 Drift and validation Split fitting and validation data, record time stamps, and repeat calibrations when drift changes the system. A numerical optimizer may fit parameters precisely even when its model is wrong. Report uncertainty, model residuals, and the actual pulse context. Characterization should support an implementation claim, not merely produce a collection of attractive numbers. Problem 7.3. Why repeat a benchmark with simultaneous neighboring operations? Worked solution Crosstalk and shared-control effects may not appear when gates are tested in isolation. The intended workload may involve parallel activity. We now apply this common language to concrete platforms, beginning with a nonlinear electrical oscillator. Sources and further study: Knill et al., Randomized benchmarking of quantum gates ."}, {"title": "Circuit Qubits", "chapter": "Superconducting Circuits", "url": "08-superconducting-circuits.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Circuit Qubits A capacitor and an inductor exchange electric and magnetic energy. Why can we not simply call their two lowest energy levels a qubit? 8.1.1 Oscillators and Josephson junctions An ideal oscillator has equally spaced levels: \\(E_n=\\hbar\\omega(n+1/2)\\). A drive resonant with the first transition also addresses higher transitions. A Josephson junction supplies a nonlinear circuit element: its energy depends on the superconducting phase difference as \\(-E_J\\cos\\varphi\\). The resulting unequal level spacings let a sufficiently weak, spectrally narrow drive select the lowest pair. 8.1.2 The transmon regime The simplified transmon Hamiltonian is \\(H=4E_C(n-n_g)^2-E_J\\cos\\varphi\\), where \\(n\\) counts excess Cooper pairs and \\(n_g\\) is an offset charge. For \\(E_J/E_C\\gg1\\), the approximate lowest transition is \\(hf_{01}\\simeq\\sqrt{8E_JE_C}-E_C\\), while \\(f_{12}-f_{01}\\simeq-E_C/h\\). Increasing this ratio suppresses charge sensitivity but leaves a weakly anharmonic oscillator; higher levels remain physically present. The Josephson nonlinearity makes the first transition distinguishable from the next. Problem 8.1. Let \\(E_C/h=0.25\\) GHz and \\(E_J/h=20\\) GHz. Estimate the lowest transition. Worked solution The estimate is \\(\\sqrt{8(20)(0.25)}-0.25=6.075\\) GHz. The next transition is approximately 0.25 GHz lower. These are large-ratio approximations."}, {"title": "Control and Coupling", "chapter": "Superconducting Circuits", "url": "08-superconducting-circuits.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Control and Coupling 8.2.1 Microwave gates A resonant microwave pulse rotates the lowest two levels, with its phase choosing an axis in the equatorial plane. A calibrated change of the drive reference implements a virtual Z rotation. Initialization combines a cold environment with waiting or active reset. Cooling alone is not a guarantee of the ground state: stray radiation and imperfect thermalization must be checked through population measurements. 8.2.2 Resonators and entangling gates Capacitive or inductive coupling permits excitation exchange; detuning and couplers help select when it occurs. Coupled resonators can mediate interactions and provide access to measurement. Different gate designs exploit different effective interactions, so an abstract CZ symbol must come with a pulse duration, leakage estimate, and calibration procedure. Driving faster can increase unwanted occupation of the second excited state. Problem 8.2. Why can a shorter pulse cause leakage? Worked solution Its spectrum is broader and its drive amplitude is usually larger. Both make an adjacent transition harder to ignore; the relevant comparison is with the anharmonicity, not the much larger carrier frequency."}, {"title": "Readout and Scaling", "chapter": "Superconducting Circuits", "url": "08-superconducting-circuits.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Readout and Scaling 8.3.1 Dispersive measurement In dispersive readout, the qubit shifts a resonator frequency. A probe acquires a state-dependent phase and amplitude, producing the two signal distributions studied in Chapter 5. In a simple two-level Jaynes–Cummings approximation, the shift scales as \\(\\chi\\simeq g^2/\\Delta\\), with angular-frequency coupling \\(g\\), detuning \\(\\Delta\\), and \\(|g/\\Delta|\\ll1\\). A transmon requires higher-level corrections; this formula is a mechanism, not a device calibration. 8.3.2 Materials, wiring, and crosstalk Dielectric loss, fluctuating frequencies, stray excitations, residual coupling, and leakage produce different signatures in the earlier characterization experiments. Scaling also adds microwave lines, cryogenic heat, frequency collisions, and simultaneous-gate crosstalk. A promising isolated qubit therefore needs a second test inside a working array. Compare gate errors with neighbouring gates active and include the cost of retuning frequencies after fabrication. Problem 8.3. If \\(g/2\\pi=10\\) MHz and \\(\\Delta/2\\pi=200\\) MHz, what is the simple dispersive estimate? Worked solution \\(\\chi/2\\pi=10^2/200=0.5\\) MHz. The ratio is 0.05; multilevel effects and probe photon number still require checking. An atomic qubit starts with naturally occurring levels instead of a fabricated nonlinear oscillator. The next chapter asks how motion supplies the missing coupling. Sources and further study: Koch et al., transmon design ."}, {"title": "Atomic Qubits", "chapter": "Trapped Ions", "url": "09-trapped-ions.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Atomic Qubits Two ions can be far enough apart to address separately and still share a collective vibration. That vibration can carry the influence of one qubit to another. 9.1.1 Trapping and internal states Electric fields confine charged atoms. The qubit resides in two selected internal states, such as hyperfine ground states or a ground and metastable state; it does not reside in the ion’s position. The trapping potential also has quantized motional levels separated by \\(\\hbar\\nu\\). Internal-state coherence and motional heating are therefore different properties of the same device. 9.1.2 Cooling and preparation Optical pumping transfers population into a chosen internal state using selection rules and spontaneous emission. Laser cooling removes motional energy. Ground-state cooling becomes especially important for gates whose approximation assumes small motion. The preparation experiment should measure both the internal population and the motional distribution; a dark ion is not, by itself, proof that both are correct. Problem 9.1. Why is an internal ground state insufficient evidence of motional cooling? Worked solution Internal and motional states are distinct degrees of freedom. Optical pumping can set the first while leaving a thermal mixture in the second. Sideband asymmetry or another motion-sensitive measurement is required."}, {"title": "Coherent Gates", "chapter": "Trapped Ions", "url": "09-trapped-ions.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 Coherent Gates 9.2.1 Carrier and sideband transitions A carrier transition changes the internal state without changing the motional quantum number. Sidebands change both: a red sideband couples \\(\\ket{0,n}\\) to \\(\\ket{1,n-1}\\). In the small-motion, resolved-sideband regime, its coupling is proportional to \\(\\eta\\Omega\\sqrt n\\), where \\(\\eta\\) measures the recoil relative to the oscillator length. The red sideband cannot lower \\(n=0\\), giving an experimental cooling diagnostic. 9.2.2 Motion-mediated entanglement A state-dependent force can move a shared vibrational mode around a closed loop in its position–momentum plane. Different spin states accumulate different phases. When the loop closes, motion disentangles from the spins while a two-qubit phase remains. This explains why returning the bus to its starting state matters: an open loop leaves which-state information in motion and reduces spin coherence. Problem 9.2. Compare red-sideband couplings for \\(n=1\\) and \\(n=4\\). Worked solution The amplitudes scale as \\(\\sqrt n\\), so the latter is twice the former under the stated approximation. The corresponding population oscillations occur twice as fast."}, {"title": "Measurement and Architecture", "chapter": "Trapped Ions", "url": "09-trapped-ions.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Measurement and Architecture 9.3.1 Fluorescence detection Illuminate a cycling transition coupled to one qubit state. Repeated fluorescence makes that state bright and the other dark. Photon-count overlap, unwanted pumping, and off-resonant scattering limit assignment fidelity. Measurement can disturb neighbouring ions, and cooling and reset take time. A circuit timing model must include those operations rather than counting laser gate pulses alone. 9.3.2 Transport and optical connections Long chains acquire many closely spaced motional modes; addressing and mode control become harder. Segmented traps transport ions between regions, while optical links can connect separate modules probabilistically. Heating, laser phase noise, intensity drift, transport excitation, and photon collection each enter a different budget. Neither moving ions nor linking modules is free: compare total schedules with coherence and link waiting times. Problem 9.3. What remains if a gate leaves distinguishable bus states? Worked solution Tracing out the bus multiplies the spin coherence by the overlap of those states. Perfectly distinguishable bus states erase that coherence, even if the spin populations look correct. Neutral atoms remove the electric charge that supports trapping and Coulomb coupling. Optical forces and highly excited electronic states replace those mechanisms. Sources and further study: Cirac and Zoller, ion computation ."}, {"title": "Atom Arrays", "chapter": "Neutral Atoms", "url": "10-neutral-atoms.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Atom Arrays An atom excited to a large electronic orbit can shift the excitation energy of its neighbour. We can use that shift to make a pulse conditional. 10.1.1 Optical traps and loading A tightly focused laser produces an optical trapping potential for a neutral atom. Arrays of such traps provide labelled sites, but loading may leave vacancies. Imaging locates occupied sites and rearrangement assembles a useful array. These operations are part of initialization: a missing atom is not simply a qubit in state zero, and occupancy must be tracked separately from internal state. 10.1.2 Internal-state encoding Long-lived internal states store the qubit, initialized by optical pumping and manipulated with microwave or Raman transitions. A Raman process uses two optical fields to drive an effective transition through an intermediate level. Detuning can reduce intermediate-state occupation, but spontaneous scattering and laser phase noise remain. Trap motion and spatially varying light shifts also affect the qubit frequency. Problem 10.1. If sites load independently with probability 0.6, what is the probability that ten prescribed sites are occupied? Worked solution It is 0.6¹⁰ ≈ 0.00605. This toy calculation explains why rearrangement can matter even when individual loading looks reasonably successful."}, {"title": "Rydberg Gates", "chapter": "Neutral Atoms", "url": "10-neutral-atoms.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Rydberg Gates 10.2.1 Excitation and blockade A laser couples \\(\\ket1\\) to a Rydberg state \\(\\ket r\\). When both atoms are excited, the interaction shifts \\(\\ket{rr}\\) by energy \\(V\\). Define the angular-frequency blockade shift \\(B=V/\\hbar\\). If \\(|B|\\gg\\Omega\\), excitation of the second atom is strongly suppressed. In a simple detuned two-level model the maximum unwanted transfer is \\(\\Omega^2/(\\Omega^2+B^2)\\); actual multilevel gate errors require a fuller model. 10.2.2 Conditional phase accumulation A representative sequence excites the control with a π pulse, applies a 2π pulse to the target transition, then de-excites the control. Whether the target completes its cycle depends on blockade. The resulting diagonal phases can realize an entangling controlled phase after single-qubit phase corrections. Follow all four computational inputs, including pulse phases, before calling the sequence CZ; blockade alone does not specify a complete gate. Problem 10.2. For \\(B=20\\Omega\\), bound transfer in the simple blockade model. Worked solution The maximum is \\(1/401\\approx0.00249\\). It is not the total error of a Rydberg gate: decay, pulse errors, and other levels remain."}, {"title": "Readout and Scaling", "chapter": "Neutral Atoms", "url": "10-neutral-atoms.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Readout and Scaling 10.3.1 Detection and atom loss Fluorescence detects atoms, and state-selective removal can convert an internal-state question into an occupancy question. Such readout may be destructive. Other schemes preserve atoms, with their own scattering and crosstalk budgets. Loss during a circuit must be distinguished from a legitimate measurement result; detecting it can supply a useful error flag but does not recover the missing quantum state automatically. 10.3.2 Rearrangement and parallel control Larger arrays require uniform illumination, stable traps, controlled Rydberg excitation, and reliable loading. Finite Rydberg lifetime penalizes slow gates, while imperfect blockade penalizes strong drives. Parallel gates can interact with one another. An architecture must specify which pairs can operate simultaneously, how vacant sites are replaced, and whether a replacement interrupts nearby stored information. Problem 10.3. Why should detected atom loss not be recorded as measurement zero? Worked solution That would merge an erasure with a valid state outcome and bias the data. Record occupancy or a separate loss flag and define how the computation handles it. In a semiconductor, electrostatic confinement plays the role of the trap, while electron spin supplies the two states. Sources and further study: Jaksch et al., neutral-atom gates ."}, {"title": "Spin Qubits", "chapter": "Semiconductor Spins", "url": "11-semiconductor-spins.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 Spin Qubits Can an electrical device use a spin to store information and charge motion only when it needs to operate on that information? 11.1.1 Confinement and Zeeman splitting Gate electrodes create a small confining potential called a quantum dot. A single electron has orbital levels and two spin states split by a magnetic field. Their separation is \\(\\Delta E=|g|\\mu_B B\\), where \\(g\\) is the material-dependent effective factor. Label the lower selected spin state zero and the higher one one; the sign of \\(g\\) determines their physical orientation. 11.1.2 Spin encodings One electron can encode one qubit. Other encodings use two or more spins to trade extra particles for different control or noise properties. Initialization may use relaxation or spin-selective tunnelling to a reservoir. The device must resolve spin and unwanted orbital or valley states sufficiently well; merely counting electrons does not establish a clean two-level computational subspace. Problem 11.1. For \\(|g|=2\\), \\(B=0.5\\) T, and \\(\\mu_B/h\\approx14\\) GHz/T, find the spin frequency. Worked solution The frequency is 2 × 14 × 0.5 = 14 GHz. This is an illustrative effective-g model, not a universal value for semiconductor devices."}, {"title": "Electrical Control", "chapter": "Semiconductor Spins", "url": "11-semiconductor-spins.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Electrical Control 11.2.1 Spin rotations A resonant oscillating magnetic field rotates a spin. Electrical driving can also move the electron through a magnetic-field gradient or exploit spin–orbit coupling, converting electrical motion into spin control. This convenience couples the qubit to charge fluctuations as well. Drive strength, detuning, heating, and leakage need the same experimental separation introduced in the control chapters. 11.2.2 Exchange gates Allowing neighbouring electron wavefunctions to overlap produces exchange. For dimensionless spin operators \\(\\mathbf S=(X,Y,Z)/2\\), write \\(H=J\\mathbf S_1\\cdot\\mathbf S_2\\), with \\(J\\) an energy. The singlet and triplet energies differ by \\(J\\). A pulse with \\(Jt/\\hbar=\\pi\\) implements SWAP up to a global phase; half that area gives an entangling square root of SWAP. Problem 11.2. If \\(J/h=10\\) MHz, how long is a SWAP exchange pulse? Worked solution \\(Jt/\\hbar=2\\pi(10^7)t=\\pi\\), so \\(t=50\\) ns; the square-root pulse takes 25 ns in the ideal constant-exchange model."}, {"title": "Readout and Integration", "chapter": "Semiconductor Spins", "url": "11-semiconductor-spins.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 Readout and Integration 11.3.1 Spin-to-charge conversion Spin-to-charge conversion maps one spin state to a different tunnelling or charge configuration. A nearby charge sensor then detects it. Pauli blockade provides another conversion mechanism in suitable double dots. The conversion can fail even if the charge detector is excellent, so quote conversion and detector errors separately. Reservoir temperature and timing also influence initialization and readout. 11.3.2 Disorder, wiring, and uniformity Charge noise changes exchange, nuclear spins can fluctuate the local magnetic field, and device disorder changes parameters from dot to dot. Material purification can address some noise sources but does not solve wiring or uniformity. Dense fabrication is useful only if gates, reservoirs, sensors, and calibration can support simultaneous operation throughout the array. Always compare usable connectivity, not just geometric density. Problem 11.3. Can perfect charge detection guarantee perfect spin readout? Worked solution No. The spin-to-charge conversion may have failed before detection. The measured assignment error combines both stages and preparation errors unless independently calibrated. A crystal defect can also localize an electronic spin, with an optical transition offering a route between stationary and flying information. Sources and further study: Loss and DiVincenzo, quantum dots ."}, {"title": "Localized States", "chapter": "Defect Centers", "url": "12-defect-centers.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 Localized States A localized electron can be a processor, a nearby nucleus a memory, and an emitted photon a connection. The useful object is then a small quantum node. 12.1.1 Defects and spin levels A defect changes the local electronic structure of a crystal. The nitrogen-vacancy center in diamond is one example with an electronic spin manifold and optical transitions. Two selected spin levels define the qubit; other levels are not removed by this choice. Magnetic fields and spectral selectivity help isolate the transition, and their stability affects the computational phase. 12.1.2 Initialization and microwave control Optical pumping can polarize the electronic spin through state-dependent relaxation pathways. Microwave pulses rotate a selected spin transition. Fluorescence contrast supplies a measurement signal, though collecting enough information may require repetition or more elaborate readout. Optical excitation also changes the environment of nearby memories; the reset procedure must be evaluated as part of the node, not only on the electron alone. Problem 12.1. Two independent required photons are each collected with probability 0.1. What is the collection contribution to success? Worked solution It is 0.1² = 0.01 before detector efficiency, protocol acceptance, or indistinguishability is included. This is a two-photon example, not a formula for every heralding protocol. Further study: Bernien et al., heralded entanglement between solid-state qubits ."}, {"title": "Spin Registers", "chapter": "Defect Centers", "url": "12-defect-centers.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Spin Registers 12.2.1 Nuclear memories A nearby nuclear spin can retain information while the electron participates in optical attempts. Hyperfine coupling makes each spin’s transition frequency depend on the other. This supplies conditional control but also means that uncontrolled electron changes can disturb the nucleus. A memory lifetime measured with an idle electron need not describe a memory exposed to repeated optical reset. 12.2.2 Conditional operations In a simplified secular two-spin model, \\(H=\\hbar A Z_eZ_n/4\\), where \\(A\\) is an angular frequency and fast nonsecular terms have been neglected. The phase of a nuclear superposition evolves differently for the two electron states. Resonant selective pulses or timed evolution can turn that difference into an entangling gate. Calibration must identify the relevant coupling and compensate unwanted single-spin phases. Problem 12.2. What is the relative conditional phase between even and odd parity under the stated interaction? Worked solution The energies are \\(\\pm\\hbar A/4\\), so their phase difference has magnitude \\(At/2\\). Single-spin phases and the sign convention must be tracked when specifying a target gate."}, {"title": "Optical Interfaces", "chapter": "Defect Centers", "url": "12-defect-centers.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 Optical Interfaces 12.3.1 Readout and heralded entanglement Photons from two nodes can interfere in a way that erases information about which node emitted them. A suitable detection event then heralds entanglement. This requires indistinguishable optical wavepackets and a specified protocol; a detector click alone is not a universal entanglement witness. Collection loss reduces success rate, while distinguishability and false detections can reduce the conditional state fidelity. 12.3.2 Collection efficiency and scaling A scalable network needs reproducible optical transitions, collection interfaces, stable interference, local memories, and control of unwanted spin interactions. Local register size and remote connection rate are separate resources. The experiment should report failed attempts and waiting time along with successful-state fidelity. Postselection cannot turn a rare high-quality event into a fast deterministic connection. Problem 12.3. Why benchmark a memory during link attempts? Worked solution Optical excitation and reset can perturb the memory. Its idle coherence time does not capture those repeated disturbances; the relevant experiment reproduces the actual network duty cycle. We next let photons carry the computation itself. Their weak interaction is both an advantage for transmission and a difficulty for gates."}, {"title": "Optical Qubits", "chapter": "Photonic Computation", "url": "13-photonic-computation.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 Optical Qubits A photon can take two paths coherently. A beam splitter mixes those paths, but making one photon control another requires an additional mechanism. 13.1.1 Encoding and sources In dual-rail encoding, \\(\\ket{0_L}=\\ket{1,0}\\) and \\(\\ket{1_L}=\\ket{0,1}\\): one photon occupies two modes. Polarization and time bins supply other encodings. A source must deliver the required photon number, spectral profile, and timing. Vacuum and extra photons are outside the intended dual-rail subspace, so source quality cannot be reduced to a qubit rotation error. 13.1.2 Interference and phase control A relative phase shifter rotates the phase between the rails, and a beam splitter mixes their amplitudes. Together they implement arbitrary single-qubit transformations in an ideal lossless two-mode model. The path lengths and optical phases must remain stable during computation. Initialization is therefore a source-and-mode preparation task, while passive optical elements provide much of the coherent one-qubit control. Problem 13.1. What happens to a dual-rail qubit if its photon is lost? Worked solution The state leaves the one-photon subspace and becomes vacuum in the two rails. This is not the logical zero, which still contains one photon."}, {"title": "Photonic Gates", "chapter": "Photonic Computation", "url": "13-photonic-computation.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Photonic Gates 13.2.1 Two-photon interference Choose the balanced beam-splitter convention \\(a^\\dagger\\mapsto(c^\\dagger+d^\\dagger)/\\sqrt2\\), \\(b^\\dagger\\mapsto(c^\\dagger-d^\\dagger)/\\sqrt2\\). One identical photon in each input produces \\((\\ket{2,0}-\\ket{0,2})/\\sqrt2\\). The coincidence terms cancel. This Hong–Ou–Mandel effect requires indistinguishability in the unmeasured degrees of freedom; distinguishable photons do not exhibit the same cancellation. 13.2.2 Measurement-induced operations Passive mode mixing alone does not provide a deterministic universal entangling gate on dual-rail qubits. Ancillary photons, interference, detection, and feed-forward can induce conditional operations. KLM establishes a route to scalable computation under idealized resource assumptions; its general construction is beyond this chapter. The central hardware question is how success probability, resource preparation, and loss affect the cost of an accepted logical operation. Problem 13.2. What is the ideal coincidence probability for one identical photon in each balanced input? Worked solution Zero: expanding the creation operators cancels the term with one photon in each output. For fully distinguishable photons the probability is one half."}, {"title": "Detection and Architecture", "chapter": "Photonic Computation", "url": "13-photonic-computation.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 Detection and Architecture 13.3.1 Loss and heralding Photon counters convert optical occupation to classical records, usually consuming the photons. Efficiency, false counts, timing resolution, and number resolution matter differently across protocols. Loss can erase a qubit before any intended measurement. Heralding can flag some failures, but discarding unsuccessful runs must be included in both the runtime and the interpretation of the reported output distribution. 13.3.2 Resource states and multiplexing Large photonic computations often prepare entangled resource states and connect them through measurements. Multiplexing chooses successful source attempts from many parallel channels, at the price of switches, delay lines, and extra loss. Thus a device with excellent interferometers can still be limited by sources and detection. A complete design counts all photons and optical storage time, including discarded resources. Problem 13.3. If independent sources succeed with probability 0.1, what is the chance that at least one of ten succeeds? Worked solution It is 1 − 0.9¹⁰ ≈ 0.6513, before switching loss. Multiplexing improves availability but does not eliminate the cost of routing the successful photon. A mode can hold more than a single photon. Using that larger space for encoding leads to bosonic error-protection strategies. Sources and further study: Knill, Laflamme, and Milburn, linear optics ."}, {"title": "Oscillator States", "chapter": "Bosonic Encodings", "url": "14-bosonic-encodings.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 Oscillator States Instead of selecting only the lowest two oscillator levels, can we distribute a qubit across many levels so that common errors leave recognizable traces? 14.1.1 Number states and coherent states An oscillator has number states \\(\\ket n\\), and photon loss acts through \\(a\\ket n=\\sqrt n\\ket{n-1}\\). A coherent state satisfies \\(a\\ket\\alpha=\\alpha\\ket\\alpha\\) and has mean occupation \\(|\\alpha|^2\\). Its number distribution is not sharp. These states describe a mode; the mode may be a microwave cavity, optical field, or motion rather than a separate hardware platform. 14.1.2 Encoding within a mode An encoding chooses a two-dimensional subspace of this larger state space. Protection requires specifying how likely physical errors move that subspace and how a measurement identifies the movement without identifying the logical amplitudes. Extra levels are useful resources only when the control and recovery operations can exploit them. High oscillator coherence alone does not establish a protected logical qubit. Problem 14.1. What does one loss do to \\(\\ket4\\)? Worked solution The unnormalized result is \\(2\\ket3\\). Conditioned on a detected loss, the normalized state is \\(\\ket3\\); the factor influences the event probability."}, {"title": "Protected Information", "chapter": "Bosonic Encodings", "url": "14-bosonic-encodings.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Protected Information 14.2.1 Cat states and parity Even and odd cat states are normalized sums \\(\\ket\\alpha\\pm\\ket{-\\alpha}\\). Expanding in number states leaves only even or odd terms, respectively. A single loss flips this parity. However, if those two cats themselves encode logical zero and one, measuring parity reads logical information. Correctable cat schemes need a specified encoding and stabilization or syndrome strategy; “measure parity” is not a universal repair instruction. 14.2.2 Grid-state encodings Grid-state encodings distribute amplitude over a lattice in position and momentum. Small displacements can then be inferred modulo the lattice spacing, preserving logical information. Ideal infinitely sharp grids require infinite energy and are mathematical limits. Finite-width peaks and a finite envelope introduce additional error probabilities. The GKP construction supplies the formal correction conditions, which are stated here rather than proved. Problem 14.2. Why can parity measurement damage a superposition of an even and an odd cat? Worked solution The two logical alternatives have different parities. Measuring parity distinguishes them and removes their coherence instead of revealing only an error syndrome."}, {"title": "Control and Recovery", "chapter": "Bosonic Encodings", "url": "14-bosonic-encodings.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 Control and Recovery 14.3.1 Auxiliary-mediated operations A two-level auxiliary system can interrogate an oscillator through occupation-dependent phase shifts. Suitable pulse sequences map a syndrome onto the auxiliary, which is measured and reset. The oscillator need not be directly measured. But auxiliary faults can propagate back to it, so syndrome extraction has its own error budget. Initialization of the encoded state and logical gates also need nonlinear control beyond a bare harmonic oscillator. 14.3.2 Finite energy and error channels For a mode with photon-loss rate \\(\\kappa\\), the short-time loss probability is approximately \\(\\kappa\\langle n\\rangle t\\). Increasing state size can improve distinguishability or suppress one error mechanism while increasing exposure to loss. Compare corrected logical performance, recovery time, and hardware resources; neither photon number nor separation in phase space alone is a measure of protection. Problem 14.3. For \\(\\kappa=100\\) s⁻¹, mean occupation 4, and \\(t=10\\) μs, estimate loss probability. Worked solution The short-time estimate is 100 × 4 × 10⁻⁵ = 0.004. Its validity requires this probability to be small. Bosonic codes engineer redundancy in oscillator space. Topological approaches seek insensitivity to local disturbances through a different structure of physical states. Sources and further study: Gottesman, Kitaev, and Preskill, oscillator encoding ; Mirrahimi et al., protected cat qubits ."}, {"title": "Nonlocal Encoding", "chapter": "Topological Approaches", "url": "15-topological-approaches.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 Nonlocal Encoding Suppose two logical states look identical to every sufficiently local probe. A local disturbance would then have difficulty learning which logical state was present. 15.1.1 Degeneracy and local perturbations Topological proposals seek a low-energy space whose logical alternatives cannot be distinguished by local operations, up to finite-size corrections. A gap separates that space from unwanted excitations. This is a conditional property of a physical model, not a consequence of naming a qubit topological. The encoding, allowed disturbances, temperature, and system size all enter the protection claim. 15.1.2 Parity and encoded information In Majorana-based proposals, spatially separated modes jointly encode fermionic occupation. A fixed total-parity constraint means that a single pair does not supply a freely controllable qubit; four ideal modes can encode one qubit within a fixed-parity sector. Logical information resides in joint parity relationships. The microscopic derivation of these modes is beyond this course; Kitaev’s wire model supplies a precise example. Problem 15.1. Why are four ideal Majorana modes used for one fixed-parity qubit? Worked solution Two fermionic occupations give four basis states. Fixing their total parity leaves two alternatives. A single pair with fixed parity leaves only one, insufficient for a qubit."}, {"title": "Topological Operations", "chapter": "Topological Approaches", "url": "15-topological-approaches.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Topological Operations 15.2.1 Exchange and braiding For suitable non-Abelian excitations, exchanging their positions transforms the degenerate state space. Different exchange orders can give different transformations. Protection requires that motion preserve the gap and avoid unwanted excitations, while finite splitting must not accumulate uncontrolled phases. A trajectory drawing by itself therefore does not establish a robust operation: the physical Hamiltonian throughout the exchange matters. 15.2.2 Readout and universality Fusion or joint-parity measurements can read encoded information and support measurement-based operations. Initialization also requires control over the relevant parity or fusion outcome. Braiding Majorana modes alone does not provide a universal gate set; additional operations or resources are needed. Their errors must be counted rather than assuming that topological protection extends automatically to a complete computation. Problem 15.2. Does a protected braid imply a universal processor? Worked solution No. The generated gate set may be restricted. State preparation, readout, and any additional non-braiding gates require independent demonstrations and error budgets."}, {"title": "Physical Requirements", "chapter": "Topological Approaches", "url": "15-topological-approaches.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 Physical Requirements 15.3.1 Gaps, poisoning, and finite size If an unwanted excitation costs energy \\(\\Delta\\), a simple equilibrium estimate contains a factor \\(e^{-\\Delta/(k_BT)}\\). A large ratio suppresses thermal occupation but says nothing by itself about nonequilibrium quasiparticles. Parity-changing events, often called poisoning, can destroy the encoding. Finite separation can also split the nominally degenerate states, producing unwanted relative phases during storage and gates. 15.3.2 Evidence and limitations Evidence must establish the claimed encoding and operations, distinguish alternative explanations of signals, and measure errors in the relevant regime. A spectral feature alone is not a demonstration of protected computation. Separate a theoretical model, evidence for its ingredients, and a working logical operation. This chapter presents design principles and their conditions; it does not assert that a complete protected universal processor has been realized. Problem 15.3. Compare Boltzmann factors for \\(\\Delta/k_BT=5\\) and 10. Worked solution They are about 0.00674 and 0.0000454. Their ratio is about 148. This calculation estimates thermal suppression only, not a measured parity lifetime. Whatever the encoding, controls, cooling, and classical processing must support it. The next chapter counts those physical requirements. Sources and further study: Kitaev, quantum wires ; Kitaev, computation by anyons ."}, {"title": "Operating Conditions", "chapter": "Control Infrastructure", "url": "16-control-infrastructure.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 Operating Conditions A gate is a timed physical signal, and a measurement is a signal that must be interpreted. The classical equipment is part of the quantum computer. 16.1.1 Cryogenics and vacuum Cooling reduces thermal occupation and supports superconducting operation where required. Vacuum reduces collisions in trapped-particle devices. Neither condition has a single universal specification: the relevant temperature or collision rate depends on the energy scale and experiment. A cold refrigerator plate does not prove that a qubit or resonator has the same effective temperature. Measure the populations or noise that matter to computation. 16.1.2 Optical and microwave delivery Cables and optical paths carry control fields but also introduce attenuation, phase drift, distortion, and unwanted radiation. Filtering can suppress noise while limiting pulse bandwidth. Optical alignment and microwave impedance matching solve different delivery problems. Characterize the signal at the device through its response, since the waveform programmed at room temperature need not be the waveform experienced by the qubit. Problem 16.1. A hypothetical cold stage has 10 μW available; each line adds 20 nW. What is the line-only upper bound? Worked solution The quotient is 500 lines. Other heat loads reduce that bound; it is not a complete refrigerator design."}, {"title": "Classical Control", "chapter": "Control Infrastructure", "url": "16-control-infrastructure.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 Classical Control 16.2.1 Waveforms and synchronization Waveform generators represent signals with finite sampling rates and resolution. Mixers, oscillators, and amplifiers translate these signals to the required frequencies. Shared clocks and phase references let separate pulses define a coherent sequence. Timing errors affect conditional operations, while reference-phase errors change rotation axes. Synchronization must include detectors and feedback channels as well as drive generation. 16.2.2 Feedback and decoding Readout digitization, discrimination, and decoding take time. A feedback operation cannot depend on a result that has not arrived. Some Pauli corrections can be tracked in a classical frame and applied through later interpretation, but subsequent noncommuting operations may require resolved information. Distinguish processing latency from throughput: a decoder can keep up on average and still have problematic response-time tails. Problem 16.2. A syndrome round takes 1 μs and decoding latency is 5 μs. Must every round wait 5 μs? Worked solution Not necessarily. A pipelined decoder can accept one round per microsecond while returning results five rounds later. Operations that require those results still face the latency, and sustained throughput must keep up."}, {"title": "System Budgets", "chapter": "Control Infrastructure", "url": "16-control-infrastructure.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 System Budgets 16.3.1 Heat, bandwidth, and wiring Every line introduces a heat and space cost. Amplifiers and classical electronics dissipate power at particular temperature stages, each with a separate cooling budget. Moving computation nearer the device can reduce wiring while increasing local heat. A useful budget lists static heat leaks, active dissipation, signal bandwidth, and channel count; a single room-temperature power total hides the most restrictive stage. 16.3.2 Calibration and stability Calibration is an ongoing workload. Frequencies, amplitudes, readout thresholds, and interaction parameters can drift during an experiment. Track when each parameter was measured and validate it against representative circuits. Increasing processor size adds simultaneous-control contexts as well as parameters. A system that spends most of its time restoring calibrations has less useful availability than its gate duration suggests. Problem 16.3. If calibration takes 15 minutes each hour, what fraction remains for data collection? Worked solution At most 45/60 = 75%, before other overhead. Useful execution estimates should include this duty cycle rather than reporting only pulse time. Infrastructure limits which operations can happen together. Architecture translates these constraints into a schedule for an entire algorithm."}, {"title": "Connectivity", "chapter": "Processor Architecture", "url": "17-processor-architecture.html#section-17-1", "text": "17.1 Connectivity The same logical circuit can require very different physical operations on different connection graphs. Architecture makes that difference explicit. 17.1.1 Local and nonlocal interactions Represent available two-qubit interactions by edges between physical qubits. An edge also has a direction if the native gate requires one, a duration, and an error model. Some pairs share a mediator or control channel and cannot operate simultaneously even when they have no common qubit. A graph is therefore the beginning of a scheduling model, not the whole architecture. 17.1.2 Routing and scheduling A gate between distant logical partners may require SWAPs, movement, or teleportation. A SWAP can be decomposed into three CNOTs when those CNOTs are available, but another platform may implement exchange directly. Track the mapping of logical states after every move; restoring the original mapping is sometimes unnecessary. The compilation objective must include idle errors and contention, not only the number of added gates. Problem 17.1. On a line A–B–C, how can an A–C interaction be made local? Worked solution Swap A and B, then interact the state now at B with C. A restoring SWAP is needed only if the later schedule requires the original placement. In a CNOT decomposition one SWAP costs three CNOTs."}, {"title": "Modular Systems", "chapter": "Processor Architecture", "url": "17-processor-architecture.html#section-17-2", "text": "17.2 Modular Systems 17.2.1 Photonic links An optical link can establish an entangled pair between modules. If independent attempts succeed with probability \\(p\\) and take time \\(\\tau\\), the mean waiting time is \\(\\tau/p\\), neglecting other overhead. This geometric waiting time has a broad distribution. Heralded availability must enter the schedule, and successful pairs may need storage while other links are being prepared. 17.2.2 Communication and memory Local memory competes with link waiting time. Parallel attempts can improve availability but need extra communication qubits, optics, and routing. Entanglement purification can improve state quality at a resource cost under suitable noise assumptions. A modular design should state which qubits communicate, which compute, how they exchange states, and what happens to the computation when a link attempt fails. Problem 17.2. A link succeeds with probability 0.02 per 10 μs attempt. Find its mean waiting time. Worked solution It is 10 μs/0.02 = 500 μs under independent trials, with no extra reset or communication delay. A memory requirement cannot be inferred from this mean alone; long waits also occur."}, {"title": "Scaling Design", "chapter": "Processor Architecture", "url": "17-processor-architecture.html#section-17-3", "text": "17.3 Scaling Design 17.3.1 Fabrication and yield If every component independently works with probability \\(y\\), the probability that all \\(N\\) work is \\(y^N\\). This simple yield model motivates spare components and defect-tolerant layouts. Real defects can be correlated, so the independent estimate is only a starting point. A large fabricated array and a large usable connected array are different achievements. 17.3.2 Architecture-aware resource counts Start a resource estimate from a compiled workload: count active qubits, communication and syndrome auxiliaries, routed gates, measurement rounds, and elapsed time. Then include failures, retries, and calibration. Compare designs at a matched target output accuracy. The architecture that minimizes physical qubits may not minimize time or total spacetime use, especially when communication or auxiliary-state preparation dominates. Problem 17.3. For 1,000 independent components of yield 0.999, estimate perfect-array yield. Worked solution It is 0.999¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 0.368. Redundancy and repairable connectivity can be essential even when individual yield is high. Error correction adds a structured workload of its own. The next chapter turns its parity checks into device-level operations."}, {"title": "Syndrome Extraction", "chapter": "Fault-Tolerant Hardware", "url": "18-fault-tolerant-hardware.html#section-18-1", "text": "18.1 Syndrome Extraction A parity measurement should reveal whether two data bits agree without revealing either bit. Implementing that distinction is the first hardware task of error correction. 18.1.1 Physical parity circuits Prepare an auxiliary in \\(\\ket0\\). Apply CNOT from data qubit 1 to the auxiliary, then from data qubit 2 to the auxiliary, and measure the auxiliary in Z. For data input \\(\\ket{x,y}\\), the recorded bit is \\(x\\oplus y\\). A superposition within one parity sector remains coherent in the ideal circuit. The measurement reveals \\(Z_1Z_2\\), not the individual Z values. 18.1.2 Measurement faults and repetition An auxiliary readout error can imitate a data error, so practical syndrome extraction repeats checks and compares their history. Auxiliary faults can also propagate through gates to several data qubits. Gate ordering, reset, and leakage handling therefore matter. Repeated measurement does not automatically make a circuit fault tolerant: the propagation of each plausible fault must be examined. The measured auxiliary records agreement or disagreement while preserving coherence within each parity sector. Problem 18.1. What happens when the parity circuit receives (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2? Worked solution Both components put the auxiliary in zero, so its measurement leaves their superposition intact in the ideal circuit. Measuring the two data qubits individually would instead reveal which component was present."}, {"title": "Logical Operations", "chapter": "Fault-Tolerant Hardware", "url": "18-fault-tolerant-hardware.html#section-18-2", "text": "18.2 Logical Operations 18.2.1 Code distance and decoding Code distance is the smallest number of physical Pauli errors that can implement a nontrivial logical operation undetected. With perfect syndrome information, a distance-d code corrects arbitrary errors on up to floor((d−1)/2) qubits. Circuit faults and noisy measurements require a spacetime decoding model. A decoder estimates a correction or a classical frame update from the measured syndrome history. 18.2.2 Gates and auxiliary states Logical gates must preserve protection during the operation. Some use transversal gates, code deformation, or measurements between encoded patches. Universal computation may require prepared auxiliary states whose production consumes substantial space and time. This chapter does not derive a universal fault-tolerant gate set; its hardware lesson is to include these resources in the workload instead of counting only data patches. Problem 18.2. How many arbitrary physical-qubit errors can a distance-5 code correct with perfect syndrome information? Worked solution At most floor((5−1)/2) = 2. This does not mean that every pair of faulty circuit locations is harmless; faults can propagate to multiple data qubits."}, {"title": "Hardware Requirements", "chapter": "Fault-Tolerant Hardware", "url": "18-fault-tolerant-hardware.html#section-18-3", "text": "18.3 Hardware Requirements 18.3.1 Leakage, correlation, and latency Leakage violates a two-level error model and can persist across many checks. Correlated faults can defeat estimates based on independent local errors. Decoder latency can delay feed-forward, while reset failures contaminate later rounds. Benchmark the complete repeating syndrome cycle, including simultaneous operations, and report logical performance as a function of distance under the same experimental conditions. 18.3.2 Thresholds and overhead A common illustrative below-threshold fit is \\(p_L\\approx A(p/p_{\\rm th})^{(d+1)/2}\\) for odd distance. Here \\(p\\) is a physical error parameter, \\(p_{\\rm th}\\) depends on the code, circuit, noise, and decoder, and \\(A\\) is a fitted prefactor. This is not a universal law or a guarantee from one average gate fidelity. A threshold theorem requires explicit noise and control assumptions. Problem 18.3. Use \\(A=0.1\\), \\(p/p_{\\rm th}=0.1\\). Compare distances 3 and 5 in the illustrative fit. Worked solution The estimates are \\(10^{-3}\\) and \\(10^{-4}\\). Increasing distance improves this model at the cost of more qubits and operations; a real estimate must validate its noise assumptions. We can now evaluate a processor through the computation it can carry out, with the full preparation, control, measurement, and correction costs included. Sources and further study: Fowler et al., surface-code circuits and resources ."}, {"title": "Performance Measures", "chapter": "Hardware Evaluation", "url": "19-hardware-evaluation.html#section-19-1", "text": "19.1 Performance Measures A processor with more qubits is not necessarily the processor that completes a specified computation more accurately or sooner. Evaluation begins by fixing the task. 19.1.1 Physical metrics Physical metrics describe components: coherence times, gate durations, leakage, readout errors, connectivity, loss, and simultaneous-operation performance. Each answers a different question. Specify the experiment and inference model behind a reported number. In particular, randomized benchmarking error, assignment error, and worst-case circuit error are not interchangeable probabilities that can simply be added into a universal score. 19.1.2 Logical metrics Logical metrics describe encoded behaviour: memory error per correction cycle, logical gate error, cycle time, and resources required at the target reliability. Compare several code distances under matched conditions when claiming error suppression. A logical memory experiment is valuable but does not establish equally reliable universal logical gates. Include the auxiliary-state production and measurement operations needed by the intended computation. Problem 19.1. Why is a long T1 insufficient to predict circuit accuracy? Worked solution Dephasing, control error, leakage, readout, and interactions can dominate even when relaxation is weak. The circuit schedule determines how much each mechanism matters."}, {"title": "Workload Evaluation", "chapter": "Hardware Evaluation", "url": "19-hardware-evaluation.html#section-19-2", "text": "19.2 Workload Evaluation 19.2.1 Compilation and execution cost Compile a representative algorithm to each architecture, then include state preparation, routing, readout, reset, retries, and calibration. For probabilistic protocols, count rejected attempts. Report both elapsed time and the number of useful outputs. A postselected high-fidelity sample is not directly comparable with an unconditional output unless acceptance and resource costs are also supplied. 19.2.2 Statistical uncertainty For independent Bernoulli outcomes, the estimated success fraction \\(\\hat p\\) has approximate standard error \\(\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)/N}\\) away from extreme probabilities. Drift and correlations invalidate the independent-trial interpretation. Zero observed failures does not establish zero error; for independent trials a one-sided 95% upper bound is \\(1-0.05^{1/N}\\), approximately \\(3/N\\). Problem 19.2. In 10,000 independent trials, 9,000 succeed. Estimate one standard error. Worked solution The estimate is √(0.9 × 0.1/10,000) = 0.003, or 0.3 percentage points. It quantifies sampling variation under the model, not calibration bias or drift."}, {"title": "Comparative Assessment", "chapter": "Hardware Evaluation", "url": "19-hardware-evaluation.html#section-19-3", "text": "19.3 Comparative Assessment 19.3.1 Matched resource models Fix an output task, acceptable error, and resource accounting before comparing platforms. One device may trade slower gates for longer coherence, another more auxiliaries for faster correction, and another loss for heralded retries. Present these tradeoffs as a table of assumptions and outcomes. Without a matched workload, a single ranking conceals the choices that determine it. 19.3.2 A complete implementation study As a final study, choose a short entangling circuit and an encoded memory experiment. Specify physical states and interactions, compile the schedules, model noise, and predict observed distributions with uncertainty. List which parameters are measured and which are assumed. Then change one bottleneck and recompute the result. This connects the course’s microscopic models to an honest system-level engineering decision. Problem 19.3. No failures occur in 1,000 independent trials. What upper error bound follows at 95% confidence? Worked solution The exact one-sided bound is 1 − 0.05^(1/1000) ≈ 0.00299. Reporting “zero error” would discard the uncertainty that the experiment actually leaves. The full cycle is now visible: a computational task determines a circuit, the circuit demands physical operations, and the physical error and resource budgets determine whether the task succeeds. Return to the algorithms and programming courses with these budgets attached to every abstract operation."}];
